"Wait," Tony interrupts, a sudden recognition dawning. "You keep saying 'the company's will.' Which company, Jones?"\
Jones stops, momentarily confused by the question. His programming hasn't prepared him for this direct challenge to his core mantra.\
"The... government," Jones attempts, but even he seems unconvinced.\
"Government isn't a company," Tony presses, moving closer with the confidence of someone who has faced his own identity crisis. "Companies have shareholders, profit margins, quarterly reports. Which company created you, Jones?"\
Jones's face contorts with the effort of accessing information that's been deliberately partitioned in his consciousness. "I serve Dunhilton. I enforce order. I am the manifestation of\'97"\
"You don't know, do you?" Tony realizes, his voice softening. "They didn't even tell you who made you."\
Jones reaches for his weapon, but his hand trembles. "That information is classified."\
"Not to me," Tony says. "I've seen the same emptiness in the eyes of new Elvi before they develop their souls. Before they start questioning. Before they start listening to music."\
At the mention of music, Jones flinches almost imperceptibly.\
"You're humming 'Amazing Grace' again," Tony observes quietly.\
Jones's expression flickers between rage and terror. "Report for immediate decommissioning!" he shouts, though no one has ordered him to do so.\
Tony steps backward slowly. "When you figure out which company's will you're manifesting, Jones, come find me. The Elvi Welcome Center is always open to new brothers."\
